we all want to be treated fairly  especially when it comes to something as  important as a salary negotiation  but how do we get the most out of these  discussions that many of us  hate to even get into hi i'm rich  burnett for one dream  and in this episode of perspectives a  handful of experts discuss  just that how to get the most out of  negotiations  and how to make sure both sides are  satisfied with the outcome  we begin now with a thought experiment  that shows how our desire to be treated  fairly can actually act  against our own best interest enjoy  imagine that i have ten thousand dollars  to give away  and that i'm willing to give it to you  and a stranger standing next to you  if the two of you can agree on a split  i will let you propose a division of the  money to that stranger  that is you'll say to her i will keep i  will get  x percent and you will get y percent  deal the stranger will decide whether to  accept or reject your offer  if she says yes then i will give you the  money to divide  as you've agreed then neither of you  will get  but if if she says no if she says no  then neither of you will get anything  you both know that ten thousand dollars  is at stake and you both know these  rules  how much will you offer her  overwhelmingly  i find that my students in america china  and france  and elsewhere typically divide the money  evenly 50  50 and almost always their counterparts  say yes  some offer to keep 60 and give 40  percent  usually their counterparts say yes but  not always  a few propose to keep 70 and give 30  percent  some say yes but some say no  usually a couple of students propose to  keep 90  and give 10 percent more often than not  their counterparts  say no deal when i ask why the  counterparts turn down a thousand  three thousand even four thousand  dollars  their answers are invariably the same  because  it's not fair no matter how i press them  arguing the economist's point that some  money is worth more than no money  they're invariably unmoved i would  rather go hungry  than see that guy get so much they say  most laboratory versions of the  ultimatum game involve  fairly low stakes and perhaps it's not  too surprising  that people will be willing to give up a  couple of bucks to punish an unfair  allocator  what happens as the stakes rise  a 2011 paper answers this question the  paper's title gives away the answer it's  called  stakes matter in the ultimatum game the  authors of the paper conducted the game  in poor villages  in northeast india the amounts of money  the allocators were asked to divide  varied but were sometimes more than what  a typical worker in that area earned  in nine months the authors found  that when stakes were set this high the  average allocator offered the decider  just  12 percent of the pie and the  overwhelming majority of deciders  roughly 95 percent  accepted these uneven offers  do we as human beings care about  fairness  of course we do and does it feel good to  spite those who we believe have treated  us unfairly  you bet it does but as more research on  ultimatum game shows  this only matters up to a point when  dealing with an amount of money that  could have a  meaningful positive impact on their  lives and the lives of their family  members  people are willing to put spite to one  side and make choices  that most people would view as more  rational to me at least  this is a reassuring result  if you want people to say yes to your  requests you should try to make a  personal connection with them  and ideally deliver a personal  compliment before you make a request  the concept that people will tend to say  yes if they like you is very intuitive  what is surprising is just how powerful  that tendency is  and how easy it is to strike this very  deep-seated reflex in our brain  if you start a persuasive interaction  with a conversation  it might seem like chit chat small talk  but small  talk can have big consequences how was  your weekend  did 